1️⃣ SMPS – 12V, 5A

Where it is used:
Mounted in a dry, protected control box.

Step-by-step Use:

Converts AC mains power into stable 12V DC.

Powers the 40W water pump reliably.

Supplies power to Arduino and other electronics (via voltage regulation if needed).

Importance:
Ensures safe, continuous operation of all electronic components.

2️⃣ Submersible DC Water Pump – 40 Watt

Where it is used:
Placed inside the nutrient tank.

Step-by-step Use:

Receives power through the relay from SMPS.

Pumps liquid nutrient solution to flow over plant roots.

Excess solution drains back into the tank (closed-loop).

Importance:
Delivers essential nutrients directly to roots — main functional component.

3️⃣ Microcontroller – Arduino

Where it is used:
Mounted on breadboard or control panel, away from water.

Step-by-step Use:

Reads data from DHT11 and pH sensor.

Determines pump operation based on timed intervals and sensor readings.

Sends control signals to relay and LCD.

Importance:
Acts as the brain of the system, enabling automation and monitoring.

4️⃣ Relay Modules – 2 Units

Where they are used:
Between Arduino and pump power line.

Step-by-step Use:

First relay switches pump ON/OFF based on Arduino command.

Second relay is reserved for future use (optional: valve, light, or backup pump).

Isolates low-power Arduino from high-current pump.

Importance:
Provides safe electrical switching and protects Arduino.

5️⃣ DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor

Where it is used:
Placed near plants, protected from splashes.

Step-by-step Use:

Measures air temperature.

Measures humidity around plants.

Sends readings to Arduino for monitoring and display.

Importance:
Allows tracking of plant-growing environment for optimization.

6️⃣ pH Sensor

Where it is used:
Probe placed inside nutrient reservoir.

Step-by-step Use:

Measures acidity/alkalinity of liquid nutrient.

Sends pH data to Arduino.

Can trigger alerts if pH is outside safe range.

Importance:
Ensures proper nutrient uptake by roots.

7️⃣ LCD Display (16×2 or 20×4)

Where it is used:
Mounted on control panel for easy monitoring.

Step-by-step Use:

Receives data from Arduino (temperature, humidity, pH, pump status).

Displays real-time values:

Example:

Temp: 28°C  Hum: 65%
pH: 6.5    Pump: ON


Alerts user if values go outside safe limits.

Importance:

Enables instant monitoring without external apps.

Shows pump status, environment, and nutrient info to users and judges.

8️⃣ Breadboard

Where it is used:
Used for assembling Arduino and sensors connections.

Step-by-step Use:

Provides a safe platform for prototyping.

Allows easy modifications without soldering.

Helps test electronics before final setup.

Importance:
Safe, flexible, and convenient for a student project.

9️⃣ Jumper Wires (As per use)

Where they are used:
Throughout the circuit.

Step-by-step Use:

Connect sensors (DHT11, pH) to Arduino.

Connect Arduino outputs to relay and LCD.

Distribute power to components as required.

Importance:
Ensures organized, reliable connections.

🔁 Overall System Sequence

SMPS powers Arduino, pump, and LCD.

Arduino reads DHT11 & pH sensor.

Arduino updates LCD display with real-time data.

Arduino controls pump ON/OFF via relay.

Liquid nutrient flows over roots and returns to tank.

Cycle repeats automatically.
